          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Are there eating habits that tend to make reflux worse?",
          "answer": "Yes. The guidance names several eating habits that are linked with worse reflux (heartburn and acid coming back up):\n\n- **Large meals.** Eating a lot at once can increase pressure in the stomach and make reflux more likely. (Source: *Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*)\n- **Late meals.** Eating close to bedtime is a common trigger, since symptoms tend to be worse when lying down. (Source: *GERD*)\n- **Fatty and fried foods.** Fat slows stomach emptying, so food stays in the stomach longer and the window for reflux gets longer. This is part of why a heavy late dinner can be a problem when the same food at lunchtime is not. (Source: *Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers*)\n- **Alcohol and carbonated drinks.** Alcohol relaxes the valve between the stomach and the food pipe and increases acid exposure. Fizzy drinks add gas that stretches the stomach. (Source: *Eating with reflux*)\n- **Other common trigger foods.** Coffee, chocolate, citrus, tomato, mint and spicy food often appear on lists of triggers. (Source: *Eating with reflux*)\n\n**Practical guidance from the retrieved material:**\n\n- **Find your own triggers rather than cutting out everything.** The guidance says removing all common trigger foods has weak evidence and is hard to keep up. A more practical approach is to write down what you eat and when, along with your symptoms, for a couple of weeks. Then remove the foods that repeatedly come before symptoms. Most people find they have two or three real triggers, and the list differs from person to person. (Source: *Eating with reflux*)\n- **Keep a food and symptom diary.** This is the same method used for other trigger-based conditions, such as migraine, because memory alone tends to pick out the memorable episodes. (Source: *Migraine*)\n- **Manage body weight if it is above the healthy range.** Weight loss has the strongest support of any lifestyle measure for reflux. (Source: *Eating with reflux*)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give detailed advice on how long to wait between eating and lying down, or on other specific positional measures, so I cannot give those details here. A clinician or dietitian can help with them.\n\n**When to see a clinician:** The guidance lists features that call for prompt medical review, including new symptoms starting at an older age and a family history of upper digestive cancer. If your symptoms are new, getting worse, or come with trouble swallowing or unexplained weight loss, speak to a doctor soon. If you have severe chest pain, pain spreading to the arm or jaw, breathlessness, or vomiting blood, seek emergency care immediately.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
